INFO:agent.loop:\u26a1 Executing tool: 'knowledge_agent' with arguments: {"source_title": "Zero-Day Vulnerability in MOVEit Transfer Exploited for Data Theft / CISA advisory page", "source_url": "https://www.cisa.gov/news-events/cybersecurity-advisories/aa23-158a", "text": "SRC-001 (Mandiant article reproduced on CISA page): Progress announced the MOVEit Transfer zero-day vulnerability on May 31, 2023; assigned CVE-2023-34362. Earliest Mandiant evidence of exploitation was May 27, 2023, with web-shell deployment and data theft, sometimes within minutes. Mandiant initially attributed the activity to UNC4857, then on June 6 merged UNC4857 into FIN11 based on targeting, infrastructure, certificate, and data-leak-site overlaps. The campaign affected organizations in Canada, India, and the U.S.; public samples suggested Italy, Pakistan, and Germany too, while impact was likely broader. Actors deployed a newly discovered C# LEMURLOOT web shell, often named human.aspx, human2.aspx, or _human2.aspx, masquerading as legitimate MOVEit component human.aspx. POSTs to guestaccess.aspx preceded web-shell interaction, indicating SQL injection directed at that file. LEMURLOOT used a hard-coded GUID-like password in X-siLock-Comment and commands via X-siLock-Step1/2/3; it could enumerate files/folders, retrieve configuration and Azure Blob storage credentials/settings, retrieve files, and create/delete a 'Health Check Service' user. Scanning/exploitation often came from 5.252.188.0/22; second-stage interaction/data theft came from different systems. Several second-stage hosts had RDP and certificates generated May 19-22, suggestive of staging. On June 5 Bleeping Computer reported a person affiliated with CLOP claimed responsibility; on June 6 the CL0P^_-LEAKS data-leak site claimed responsibility and threatened to publish stolen data unless victims paid. Mandiant called the campaign consistent with extortion actors but had not directly observed extortion emails to confirmed victims at publication. Mandiant identified overlaps in ISPs, netblocks, IPs with historical FIN11 operations; an exploitation IP had been used by FIN11 since mid-January 2023, hosting RDP on TCP 3389 and retaining the same x509 certificate when UNC4857 used it. Mandiant assessed activity as reminiscent of prior FIN11-attributed data-theft/extortion campaigns involving Accellion FTA/DEWMODE and GoAnywhere MFT CVE-2023-0669, and said FIN11 attribution was based on the overlaps. LEMURLOOT samples appeared on VirusTotal beginning May 28, 2023. The article says extortion emails had not yet been directly observed and that delayed emails were plausible based on prior FIN11 behavior, not confirmed in this campaign."}
